1. Introduction

MediBot is a console-based Expert System built entirely in SWI-Prolog that diagnoses nine common medical conditions based on user-reported symptoms and generates a structured, prioritised treatment action plan.

The system is inspired by MYCIN (Stanford, 1972), one of the first AI programs to diagnose bacterial infections and the foundational work in medical expert systems.

The primary goal of this project was to gain practical experience with core Artificial Intelligence concepts specifically Knowledge Representation, Rule-Based Inference, and Expert System Architecture using Prolog as the implementation language.

Prolog's logical programming paradigm makes it a natural fit for encoding human expert knowledge as IF-THEN rules and querying that knowledge base through backtracking search.

MediBot operates through a yes/no symptom questionnaire covering 14 symptoms. Confirmed symptoms are stored as dynamic Prolog facts, which the inference engine then matches against a knowledge base of Horn clause rules to identify the most probable condition. A separate treatment knowledge base then delivers the recommended action plan.

2. Problem Statement

Access to qualified medical professionals is uneven across India, particularly in rural and semi-urban areas. Many people rely on unreliable internet searches or word-of-mouth to interpret their symptoms, leading to delayed treatment or dangerous self-medication.

There is a practical need for a lightweight, intelligent system that can:

• Guide users through a structured symptom assessment without requiring internet connectivity.

• Map reported symptoms to probable conditions using validated clinical logic.

• Recommend a safe, prioritised action plan including flagging when a condition is an emergency.

• Store expert medical knowledge in a form that is transparent, auditable, and extendable.

MediBot addresses these challenges by encoding a doctor's diagnostic reasoning directly as Prolog rules, making the system's logic readable, modifiable, and completely open, unlike black-box machine learning models.

3. Functional Requirements

4. Symptom Collection: Prompt the user with 14 yes/no questions covering common symptoms and store confirmed answers as dynamic facts.

5. Diagnosis Inference: Match the collected symptoms against a rule base of 9 conditions using Horn clause logic and Negation as Failure (NAF).

6. Treatment Recommendation: For each diagnosed condition, output a numbered, tiered action plan categorised by urgency.

7. Graceful Fallback: If no rule matches the given symptom set, inform the user that no specific diagnosis could be made and advise consulting a doctor.

8. Modular Knowledge Base: Keep diagnostic rules and treatment plans in separate .pl files so either can be extended without modifying the inference engine.

9. Session Cleanup: Retract all dynamic symptom facts between sessions to prevent cross-contamination of results.

10. Non-Functional Requirements

11. Performance: The system responds instantly to user input. Prolog's in-memory fact matching is O(n) over the small rule set and imperceptibly fast.

12. Usability: The CLI interface uses plain English prompts (yes./no.) and clearly labelled output sections, making it accessible to non-technical users.

13. Maintainability: The three-file architecture separates concerns cleanly. A new disease can be added by writing one diagnose/1 rule and one treat/1 clause without touching any other file.

14. Portability: SWI-Prolog is freely available on Linux, Windows, and macOS. The project has no dependencies beyond a standard Prolog installation.

15. Transparency: Because the knowledge base is written in readable Horn clauses, every diagnostic decision the system makes can be traced and verified by a human expert.

16. Safety: All treatment plans include a disclaimer that the system is for educational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice.

17. System Architecture

MediBot follows a three-layer expert system architecture, which cleanly separates the user interface, reasoning engine, and knowledge storage.

Presentation Layer:
Component: Command-Line Interface (CLI)
Key Predicate: start_diagnosis/0
File: main.pl

Business Logic Layer:
Component: Inference Engine + Input Handler
Key Predicates: ask/1, diagnose/1
Files: main.pl + kb_symptoms.pl

Knowledge Base Layer:
Component: Symptom Rules
Key Predicates: diagnose/1, symptom_list/1
File: kb_symptoms.pl

The application operates on an in-memory working memory model during runtime.

User-confirmed symptoms are stored using assertz/1 as dynamic Prolog facts, consumed by the inference engine, and then retracted at session end.

There is no persistent database. Knowledge is entirely encoded in the .pl source files.

